  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Why confidence is not a guess",
      "items": [
        "high: a standard tag for this concept, reported by the company itself",
        "medium: a retired tag, another rulebook's tag, or a total we had to reassemble",
        "low: a judgement call made for one company, flagged so it never reads as a sector rule",
        "derived: computed from other concepts, so the arithmetic is exact and the inputs carry their own confidence"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "What the evidence verdict means",
      "items": [
        "confirmed: that filing's own arithmetic does not contradict the mapping",
        "equivalent: the filer calls the tag a part of our total, and its figures make the part equal that total",
        "contradicted: the filer calls it a part and its own figures make the part smaller. A mapping worth reading twice",
        "unexamined: that filing carries no calculation linkbase, which is true of most quarterly reports"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Two traps this shape avoids",
      "items": [
        "Being declared a part is not a contradiction on its own: where a company has no noncontrolling interest the part and the total carry the same number, so the figures are compared per period, not the shape of the arc",
        "US GAAP is preferred over IFRS deliberately rather than by filing date: one company files both for the same year, and the later filing is not always the right one"
      ]
    }
  ]
